+/*
+ * Explicit mm hooks instead of the generic <asm-generic/mm_hooks.h> — this
+ * arch needs a REAL arch_dup_mmap(), not its no-op default.
+ *
+ * #192 fork use-after-free this closes: dup_mmap() (mm/mmap.c) runs
+ * mm_init() — which calls init_new_context() and so zeroes the CHILD's
+ * mm->context.image — before it duplicates the VMAs/page tables and finally
+ * calls arch_dup_mmap(). With the generic no-op hook, the child's
+ * mm->context.image stayed NULL forever: nothing wrong yet, because the
+ * child's mm->start_code/end_code were copied from the parent's VMA and
+ * still point at the PARENT's cached buffer bytes — but nothing had taken a
+ * reference on the child's behalf. A parent that exited (dropping its own
+ * reference via destroy_context(), which could free the buffer outright if
+ * the cache had already evicted its slot) before the child's first run or a
+ * later dlopen left the child's mm pointing at freed pages — a genuine
+ * lazy-read-after-free, not merely a leak. Taking an explicit reference here
+ * makes the child's mm a full, independent owner from the moment dup_mmap()
+ * returns, exactly like every other refcounted resource a fork duplicates.
