--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/wasm/mm/exec_image.c
@@ -0,0 +1,521 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * Software-MMU exec image cache (#192).
+ *
+ * Problem this exists to fix: under CONFIG_MMU the engine cannot read a
+ * process's wasm bytes out of a demand-paged USER mapping (see the design
+ * comment in asm/mmu.h), so fs/binfmt_wasm.c keeps a physically-contiguous
+ * KERNEL copy alive for the mm's whole lifetime instead — one
+ * alloc_pages_exact() + kernel_read() per exec, freed by destroy_context()
+ * when that mm dies. That is correct in isolation, but a real workload
+ * re-execs the SAME on-disk binary over and over (an autoconf `configure`
+ * loop invokes the ~57 MB clang.wasm dozens of times in one boot): each exec
+ * throws its predecessor's identical buffer away and allocates a fresh
+ * order-14+ block, and the alloc/free churn fragments this arch's buddy heap
+ * (CONFIG_ARCH_FORCE_MAX_ORDER-sized blocks, no hardware MMU to make
+ * fragmentation invisible) until an alloc fails outright — an Nth-exec
+ * cliff, empirically the 5th-7th large exec in a row (#192).
+ *
+ * Fix: cache the buffer by the identity of the FILE, refcounted, so every
+ * exec of the same on-disk bytes shares one allocation instead of pounding
+ * the allocator once per exec. Concretely this also lets a compiler driver +
+ * its cc1-equivalent share one buffer concurrently (halving the peak for
+ * that pair) and skips a ~57 MB squashfs-zstd re-decompression on every
+ * cache hit.
+ *
+ * KNOWN TRADEOFF: moving the load ahead of begin_new_exec() (see
+ * wasm_exec_image_get()'s call site in fs/binfmt_wasm.c) means, for a
+ * DIFFERENT-binary exec, the OLD mm's image and the NEW exec's freshly
+ * loaded/looked-up image can be resident SIMULTANEOUSLY for a moment (the
+ * old mm is only torn down once the new one is actually installed) — before
+ * this cache existed, the old mm's buffer was already gone by the time a new
+ * one was requested. So "halving the peak" (the driver+cc1 sharing point
+ * above) is a same-file property; a different-file exec can raise the
+ * transient peak versus the old code. Two things bound that in practice:
+ * (1) the pre-point-of-no-return placement turns a transient shortage into a
+ * clean execve() == -ENOMEM instead of a kill, and (2) the evict-and-retry
+ * path below (a failed alloc synchronously frees idle cache entries and
+ * retries once) is exactly the backstop for this raised peak — an idle
+ * predecessor image doesn't have to wait for the shrinker to be reclaimed.
+ *
+ * Identity + validators. See the struct comment in asm/exec_image.h for the
+ * full accounting of what (s_dev, i_ino) does and does NOT prove on this
+ * guest (32-bit overlayfs without xino discards the layer fsid, so it is a
+ * hash bucket, not an identity proof) and why size/mtime/i_version are weak
+ * or dormant validators here (fixed-length /nix store paths, Nix's
+ * normalized-to-epoch mtime, no fs on this guest setting SB_I_VERSION). The
+ * validator that actually carries the correctness burden is the memcmp
+ * against the caller's bprm->buf in wasm_exec_image_get() below.
+ *
+ * Locking. Two locks, deliberately not one:
+ *
+ *   - wasm_exec_image_lock (spinlock) protects the LRU LIST ONLY —
+ *     insertion, removal, and refcount bumps done while walking it. It is
+ *     NEVER held across alloc_pages_exact()/kernel_read(): both can sleep
+ *     (kernel_read blocks on I/O), and alloc_pages_exact() for an order-14+
+ *     request can enter direct reclaim, which is precisely what the
+ *     shrinker below wants to be free to run — a shrinker callback that
+ *     tried to retake a lock its own allocation was holding would deadlock.
+ *
+ *   - wasm_exec_image_load_mutex serializes the MISS path (alloc + read +
+ *     insert) so two concurrent execs of a not-yet-cached file don't both
+ *     allocate and read the same bytes and race to insert two entries for
+ *     one identity. A sleeping lock is fine here: patches/kernel/0004 pins
+ *     every user task to a single CPU, so this only ever delays a second,
+ *     genuinely-concurrent miss (two DIFFERENT large binaries exec'd at
+ *     once) — cache HITS never touch it (see the lock-free-fast-path
+ *     comment in wasm_exec_image_get()).
+ *
+ * Reclaim — two independent mechanisms, deliberately NOT relying on the
+ * shrinker alone:
+ *
+ *   1. SYNCHRONOUS evict-and-retry (wasm_exec_image_evict_idle(), called
+ *      from the miss path on an alloc_pages_exact() failure): the direct,
+ *      heuristic-independent realization of the design intent. do_shrink_
+ *      slab's math (freeable ~= a 57 MB image's page count / seeks=2 /
+ *      batch) means the registered shrinker is only reachable at moderate-
+ *      to-high reclaim PRIORITY, and page-reclaim's own early-exit checks
+ *      (nr_to_reclaim trivially satisfied from ordinary page cache,
+ *      compaction_ready() bailing for a costly high-order request) can let
+ *      direct reclaim give up at DEF_PRIORITY long before the shrinker is
+ *      ever invoked — i.e. the very alloc this cache exists to serve can
+ *      fail with a perfectly good idle 57 MB image sitting unreclaimed in
+ *      the cache. So the miss path does not wait for the reclaim heuristics
+ *      to maybe find it: it evicts idle entries LRU-first ITSELF, exactly
+ *      when (and only when) an allocation actually needs the memory back,
+ *      and retries the allocation once.
+ *   2. A byte-budget CAP enforced at insert (wasm_exec_image_evict_idle()
+ *      again, called after every successful insert): bounds steady-state
+ *      retention even with NO memory pressure at all. Without a cap,
+ *      distinct large binaries execed over a session (clang, wasm-ld, a
+ *      locally built a.out, …) would accumulate unboundedly — cheap on a
+ *      real 64 GB devbox, not on a ~2 GiB guest.
+ *   3. The registered shrinker (count/scan_objects) is kept as best-effort
+ *      BACKGROUND reclaim under system-wide memory pressure from OTHER
+ *      allocations (not this cache's own) — genuinely useful, but neither
+ *      (1) nor (2) depends on it ever actually being invoked. batch=1 (not
+ *      the default) so its own total_scan accounting can still cross the
+ *      scan threshold at a moderate priority instead of needing an
+ *      artificially large freeable count first.
+ *
+ * Both (1) and (2) evict refcount==1 (cache-only-referenced, i.e. idle)
+ * entries only, LRU-first — an entry pinned by a live mm is exactly as
+ * unreclaimable as that mm's mapped text on real Linux, cap or no cap.
+ */
+#include <linux/err.h>
+#include <linux/errno.h>
+#include <linux/fs.h>
